  "evidence": "TEXT_E: commitments to eliminate most of the structural deficit within a Parliament; create the Office for Budget Responsibility; immediate £6bn savings; modify National Insurance and lower/simplify business taxes; a single Work Programme, apprenticeships expansion, and NI holiday for new firms; real‑terms NHS funding growth and patient choice; an annual limit on non‑EU economic migration; tougher crime measures and elected police oversight; transparency (publish spending, recall MPs); infrastructure like high‑speed rail and superfast broadband.\nTEXT_C: immediate EU withdrawal and end to most immigration (including a halt from Muslim‑majority countries); end foreign aid; reintroduce corporal and capital punishment; raise motorway speed limit to 90 mph; nationalise telecoms for universal 100 Mbps; reverse rail privatisation; compulsory community service for school leavers; large defence spending increases.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate in 2010 prioritized the economy, jobs, the NHS, immigration controls, and crime, which TEXT_E directly addresses with detailed, actionable policies on deficit reduction, employment support, health funding, and moderated immigration caps. TEXT_C emphasizes radical positions like immediate EU exit, sweeping immigration bans, and reintroducing capital punishment, which are less aligned with broad, mainstream voter priorities at that time.",
